        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> ```\r\n> ( text ) ( other ( text ) )\r\n> ^ ^  ^ ^ ^ ^     ^ ^  ^ ^ ^\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> You could imagine finding the 4 keypoints (al, il, ir, ar) of all textobjects in the buffer, deduplicating, and then `][` keys would go to the closest next or previous keypoint\r\n\r\nI see know, thanks!\r\n\r\nWell, if you want to primarily use `][`, then it is the job for 'mini.bracketed'. It does have a [`MiniBracketed.advance()` helper](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L596) which can help here. You'd need to write your own iterator, possibly with help of 'mini.ai'.\r\n\r\nI'll think about it, but at the moment I don't see a good way of adding this type of functionality in any of 'mini.nvim' modules.",
